\chapter{Virtual Memory Architecture}

\section{Page Clustering}

Page clustering divorces the minimum MMU/TLB mapping granularity $K$
(for \foreignlanguage{german}{Körnung}) from the kernel allocation unit
$M$ (for \foreignlanguage{german}{Maßeinheit}).

\section{\texttt{LLFree}}

Page sizes $\sigma$ (for \foreignlanguage{german}{Seitengröße}) tend to
follow a pattern like
\begin{equation}
	\sigma_n = K\cdot 2^{W\cdot n}
\end{equation}
where $W$ is for \foreignlanguage{german}{Sprungweite}.
\\
The LLM GenAI Proof of Concept code for Telix demonstrated fully
lockfree \texttt{LLFree} with in-band metadata.

\section{Superpaging}

For a superpage size $\sigma_n\geq M$, allocating via \texttt{LLFree}
can provide pages of the size needed. Something beautiful happens when
$\sigma_n<M$. A kernel allocation unit can be mapped out with the
largest superpage sizes possible within the constraints of the virtual
window and the allocation unit.



